# Testing & QA Skills (2025–2026)

## How to Use This File

This file catalogs 20 foundational-to-advanced skills specific to testing and quality assurance within the Laravel ecosystem. Each skill includes a concise definition, a real-world usage trigger, and a source reference.

When assisting users, match their request to the most relevant skill name and cite it when explaining recommendations. Use these skills as the foundation before referencing advanced CI/CD or performance patterns.

Prefer Laravel 13 and Pest 3 idioms for new test suites; when editing legacy test suites, respect existing PHPUnit conventions and note migration paths where appropriate.

Source URLs point to the Laravel 13.x docs because they are forward-cumulative — the same pages describe behavior present in 11.x and 12.x, with version-specific features clearly tagged (e.g., "Laravel 13+"). When working in an older Laravel version, the linked documentation still applies; only the version-tagged features require an upgrade.

## Skill Catalog

- **PHPUnit & Pest 3 Structure** — organizes tests into descriptive suites with expressive assertion syntax and lifecycle hooks. *Use when:* verifying business logic, preventing regressions, or documenting expected behavior. *Source:* https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/testing
- **Test-Driven Development (TDD)** — writes failing tests first, then implements only enough production code to make them pass. *Use when:* clarifying ambiguous requirements, designing testable interfaces, or building a regression safety net from day one. *Source:* training
- **Feature vs Unit Test Scope** — distinguishes HTTP-level integration tests from isolated class or method verification. *Use when:* balancing test speed versus coverage, mocking external dependencies, or validating user journeys. *Source:* https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/testing#feature-tests
- **HTTP Test Helpers** — simulates requests with authentication, headers, and payloads for controller validation. *Use when:* validating API endpoints, testing middleware effects, or asserting response structures. *Source:* https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/http-tests
- **Database Testing with RefreshDatabase** — wraps each test in transactions to isolate data changes and reset state. *Use when:* testing model interactions, validating migrations, or ensuring test independence. *Source:* https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/database-testing
- **Mocking with Mockery** — creates test doubles for dependencies with expectation verification and argument matching. *Use when:* isolating units under test, simulating external services, or verifying collaboration protocols. *Source:* https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/mocking
- **Faking Facades (Mail, Queue, Event, Http)** — replaces side-effect services with in-memory spies for assertion without execution. *Use when:* testing notification logic, validating job dispatch, or asserting HTTP client usage. *Source:* https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/mocking#facades
- **Factories with States & Sequences** — defines reusable model blueprints with variations for different test scenarios. *Use when:* generating realistic test data, covering edge cases, or reducing fixture boilerplate. *Source:* https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/database-testing#defining-model-factories
- **Parallel Test Execution** — runs test suites concurrently across multiple processes to reduce total execution time. *Use when:* scaling CI pipelines, handling large test suites, or improving developer feedback loops. *Source:* https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/testing#running-tests-in-parallel
- **Code Coverage Reporting** — measures which lines and branches are exercised by tests to identify untested code. *Use when:* enforcing quality gates, prioritizing test writing, or meeting compliance requirements. *Source:* training
- **Mutation Testing with Infection** — introduces small code changes to verify tests fail, ensuring test effectiveness. *Use when:* improving test quality, detecting weak assertions, or justifying coverage metrics. *Source:* https://infection.github.io
- **Pest Architecture Testing** — declares enforceable rules about layering, namespaces, and dependencies (e.g., controllers must not touch the database directly). *Use when:* protecting hexagonal/service-layer boundaries, preventing accidental coupling, or codifying ADR decisions as executable tests. *Source:* https://pestphp.com/docs/arch-testing
- **PHPStan/Larastan Static Analysis** — performs type inference and rule checking to catch errors before runtime execution. *Use when:* preventing null pointer exceptions, enforcing strict types, or enabling safe refactoring. *Source:* https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/static-analysis
- **Laravel Pint Code Style Enforcement** — auto-formats PHP code to match framework conventions via CLI or pre-commit hook. *Use when:* maintaining team consistency, reducing PR review noise, or onboarding new developers. *Source:* https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/pint
- **PHP CS Fixer & Rector for Automation** — applies configurable rules for code style and automated version-safe refactoring. *Use when:* modernizing legacy code, enforcing architectural patterns, or preparing for framework upgrades. *Source:* training
- **CI Quality Gates** — configures pipelines to block merges on test failure, lint errors, or coverage thresholds. *Use when:* maintaining code health, preventing regressions, or enforcing team standards. *Source:* training
- **Browser Testing with Laravel Dusk** — automates real browser interactions for JavaScript-heavy feature validation. *Use when:* testing Livewire/Inertia apps, verifying authentication flows, or capturing screenshots on failure. *Source:* https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/dusk
- **Contract Testing for APIs** — validates that API providers and consumers adhere to agreed-upon request/response schemas. *Use when:* managing microservice boundaries, preventing breaking changes, or enabling independent deployment. *Source:* training
- **Performance Regression Testing** — benchmarks critical paths to detect slowdowns introduced by recent code changes. *Use when:* optimizing queries, validating caching strategies, or ensuring SLA compliance. *Source:* training
- **Security Scanning in CI** — integrates dependency CVE checks and static security analysis into pull request workflows. *Use when:* preventing vulnerable dependencies, enforcing secure coding practices, or meeting compliance audits. *Source:* training

